---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const circleHref = toolPathBySlug('unit-circle-calculator');
const quadraticHref = toolPathBySlug('quadratic-equation-solver');
const fractionHref = toolPathBySlug('fraction-calculator');
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>How do you solve a triangle from three parts? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      You need exactly three of the six parts, and they cannot all be angles. Sides are named
      a, b, and c. The angle opposite each side uses the same letter in capitals. Fill three
      boxes in the triangle calculator and leave the rest blank. The missing sides and angles
      come back as cards, and the figure is drawn with the same scale on both axes so a right
      angle looks square.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three sides is SSS. Two sides and the angle between them is SAS. Two angles and the side
      between them is ASA. Two angles and a side that is not between them is AAS. Two sides and
      an angle that is not between them is SSA, and that last pattern can fit two triangles.
      The page names which pattern it used, so you can match the result to the line on the sheet.
    </p>
    <p>
      A chat reply can quote the formulas. It will not keep both SSA figures on one scale while
      you change a side and watch the second triangle appear or vanish. That is the job of this
      page. The arithmetic stays in the browser. Nothing is uploaded.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open the Triangle Solver →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="cosines">
    <h2>When do you use the law of cosines? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Use the law of cosines when you know all three sides, or two sides and the included angle.
      For sides a, b, and c, the angle opposite a satisfies
      cos A = (b² + c² − a²) / (2bc). The same pattern, with the letters rotated, gives B and C.
      Once one angle is known from three sides, the other two follow, and they must add to 180
      degrees with the first.
    </p>
    <p>
      SAS is the same identity rearranged. If you know sides a and b and the included angle C,
      the missing side is c² = a² + b² − 2ab cos C. Try a = 3, b = 4, and C = 90 degrees. Cosine
      of 90 is 0, so the last term drops out and c is 5. That is the familiar 3-4-5 triangle,
      reached from two sides and the right angle instead of from three lengths.
    </p>
    <p>
      The law of cosines does not produce a second candidate. Cosine is one-to-one between 0 and
      180 degrees, so each valid side triple has one angle at each vertex. If the three lengths
      cannot meet, the formula is never reached. Each side must be shorter than the other two
      added together. Sides 2, 3, and 6 fail that test, and the page says so instead of printing
      a nonsense angle.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="sines">
    <h2>When do you use the law of sines? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Use the law of sines when you already know an angle and the side opposite it, and you need
      another pair. The rule is a / sin A = b / sin B = c / sin C. ASA and AAS both start by
      finding the third angle, because the three angles of a triangle add to 180 degrees. With
      three angles and one side, every other side is a scale factor times the sine of its angle.
    </p>
    <p>
      Try angles A = 40 degrees and B = 60 degrees, with side c = 10 between them. That is ASA.
      Angle C is 80 degrees. Side a is then 10 times sin 40 / sin 80, and side b is 10 times
      sin 60 / sin 80. If the known side had been a instead of c, the same angles would be AAS:
      the known side sits opposite a known angle, not between the two known angles. The numbers
      change. The method does not.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two angles that already add to 180 degrees or more leave no room for the third. The page
      stops there. It will not invent a negative angle or a side of length zero to force a
      picture onto the screen.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="ambiguous">
    <h2>What is the ambiguous SSA case? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      SSA means two sides and an angle that is not sandwiched between them. Sine of an acute
      angle equals sine of its supplement, so the law of sines can return two legal angles.
      Drop a perpendicular from the third vertex to the side of the given angle. Call that
      height h. If the opposite side is shorter than h, it never reaches, and there is no
      triangle. If it equals h, there is one right triangle. If it is longer than the other
      given side, there is one triangle. Between those, there are two.
    </p>
    <p>
      The standard classroom numbers are angle A = 30 degrees, opposite side a = 7, and other
      side b = 10. The height is b times sin 30, which is 5. Side a is longer than 5 and shorter
      than 10, so both answers survive. One angle B is about 45.585 degrees. The other is about
      134.42 degrees. The two angle C values add to 120 degrees, because each is what remains
      after 30 degrees and its own angle B. Both figures share side a and side b. Side c differs.
    </p>
    <p>
      A solver that prints only the acute angle is the failure this page is built to show. The
      second triangle is named in a caution line, and it is drawn dashed on the same axes. When
      the inputs allow only one triangle, that line stays quiet. An obtuse given angle cannot
      be ambiguous: the opposite side has to be the longest side, or there is no triangle at all.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Both triangles, or neither">
      <p>
        If the height test says two triangles fit, both belong in the answer. Hiding the obtuse
        one is how a correct-looking decimal still fails the homework.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="pythagorean">
    <h2>When does the Pythagorean theorem apply? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The Pythagorean theorem applies only when one angle is 90 degrees. Then the square of the
      hypotenuse equals the squares of the other two sides. Sides 3, 4, and 5 satisfy it, and so
      do 5, 12, and 13, and 8, 15, and 17. The right angle sits opposite the longest side. Enter
      3, 4, and 5 with the angles blank and the card for the angle opposite 5 reads 90 degrees.
    </p>
    <p>
      A near miss is not a right triangle. Sides 3, 4, and 6 form a triangle, because each side
      is shorter than the other two combined, but 9 + 16 is not 36. The angle opposite 6 is
      obtuse. The law of cosines still solves it. The Pythagorean theorem does not, and the page
      will not label that figure as right.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you know the right angle and one side, you still need a second side or a second angle
      before the third part is determined. A lone hypotenuse is a family of right triangles, not
      one of them. Add the other leg, or add an acute angle, and the rest follows.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, check a 3-4-5 triangle against the Pythagorean theorem. Checking a 3-4-5
      layout on a small build is that test before trusting a square corner. Input: sides 3, 4,
      and 5. Output: the angle opposite 5 is 90 degrees. A Pythagorean theorem calculator is
      that check. A law of cosines calculator takes over when the angle is not 90. A law of
      sines calculator takes over once an opposite pair is known. Law of sines versus law of
      cosines is which pair you already have. One SSA triangle versus two is the ambiguous
      case calculator. An SSS SAS ASA triangle solver covers the single-figure patterns. To
      solve triangle work you still bring three parts.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Three angles and no side">
      <p>
        A 30-60-90 triangle can be any size. The angles match a tiny one and a huge one. Clear
        one angle, type a side, and the scale is fixed. Until then the page refuses, on purpose.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Keeping only the acute SSA answer">
      <p>
        Angle A = 30, side a = 7, side b = 10 has two triangles. Copy both angle sets onto the
        sheet. The dashed figure is the one a single arcsin call drops.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Swapping opposite pairs">
      <p>
        Side a is opposite angle A, not "the first side I measured." If the diagram uses different
        letters, translate the opposite pairs before you type. The solver trusts the labels.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Degrees, not radians">
      <p>
        Every angle box is degrees. A right angle is 90. If the notes say pi/6, that is 30 degrees.
        The <a href={circleHref}>unit circle</a> will plot the converted angle if you want a check.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="next">
    <h2>What should you open next? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      After the triangle is solved, an angle in radians is easier to trust on the
      <a href={circleHref}>unit circle</a>. A quadratic that showed up in the same problem set
      belongs in the <a href={quadraticHref}>quadratic equation solver</a>. A side ratio you want
      to keep exact, such as 3/5 instead of 0.6, belongs in the
      <a href={fractionHref}>fraction calculator</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page does not compute area, and it does not accept a triangle that is only a shape.
      Bring three parts that fix the size, read both answers when SSA is ambiguous, and leave
      the fourth box empty.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Solve a 3-4-5 triangle →</a>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  }
</style>
